\title{\vspace{-2em}\bfseries What phase warping buys the Figure-1 canceller\\
\large non-causal and one-beat-delay, with and without warp}
\author{Working note for the CFA ear-EEG paper}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}
\maketitle
\vspace{-1.5em}

\noindent
\textbf{What was done.} Figure~1 of the paper is produced by
\texttt{ekg\_ilc\_rt.ilc\_clean(mode="noncausal")}, whose template is indexed by
\emph{fixed lag} from the R-peak. That function now takes a warp exponent $\gamma$: the
template bin for a sample at offset $p$ from beat $k$ is
$\mathrm{ra} + \mathrm{round}(p/w_k)$ with $w_k = (T_k/\bar T)^{\gamma}$, so $\gamma=0$ is the
original code path---verified bit-identical---and $\gamma=1$ stretches the template to each beat,
i.e.\ normalized cardiac phase. All four combinations of
$\{\text{non-causal},\ \text{one-beat delay}\}\times\{\gamma=0,\ \gamma=1\}$ were run, plus
$\gamma=1/2$.

\textbf{The short answer.} It depends entirely on whether the artifact is time-locked to the
R-peak or rides on cardiac phase, and nothing else. On synthetic data where that is controlled,
warping costs up to @@SYNCOST@@~dB in the first regime and buys up to @@SYNGAIN@@~dB in the second,
in both cases growing with heart-rate variability. On the real recordings the sign is not uniform,
and it is predictable: the three records whose R-locked energy sits in the QRS lobe lose a little
from warping, while the one record whose energy sits late gains clearly. Figure~1 is drawn on a
lobe-dominated record, so it loses nothing by omitting the warp. One beat of latency costs about
@@LATCOST@@~dB against the batch average, and that cost is essentially independent of $\gamma$: the
two design choices are separable.

\section{Why the comparison needs care on real data}

There is no ground truth on the real recordings, and the two obvious ground-truth-free metrics are
both traps at $\gamma=0$.

Subtracting a non-causal template from the very beats that built it drives the R-triggered average
of the residual to \emph{exactly} zero, because the template \emph{is} that average. Scored that
way the fixed-lag arm reads $-200$~dB and the comparison is meaningless. Splitting the beats in
half---template from one half, applied to the other---fixes the estimator but not the metric: the
R-triggered average of the residual over \emph{all} beats is still identically zero, since the two
half-templates average back to the full one.

We therefore (i) hold out properly, partitioning samples by their nearest beat so that every sample
is cleaned by a template that excludes its own beat, and (ii) score by variance reduction inside
the beat window rather than by R-triggered energy. Variance reduction is coordinate-free, so it
privileges neither $\gamma$, and both arms have the same number of template bins, so the comparison
is not confounded by degrees of freedom either. The one-beat-delay arm needs no split: it subtracts
before it updates, so each bin is already scored against beats it has not seen.

\section{Synthetic, with ground truth}

The shipped generator \texttt{gen\_eeg} places a \emph{fixed-shape} PQRST at every beat, so its
artifact is strictly time-locked and $\gamma=1$ cannot help by construction. Reporting only that
would be rigging the answer, so we also run a stretched variant in which each beat's waveform is
scaled in time by $T_k/\bar T$, where $\gamma=1$ is correct by construction. The two bracket
reality.

