\section{Reading}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em,itemsep=0.25em]
\item \textbf{$\gamma$ is not a free win.} Warping a template that was already aligned actively
hurts, and the damage grows with the very RR variability that motivated warping in the first place.
\item \textbf{The sign is predictable from where the artifact sits.} Stretching by $T_k/\bar T$
displaces bin $o$ by $(o-\mathrm{ra})(T_k/\bar T - 1)$ samples: essentially zero at the R-peak,
large late in the cycle. So warping cannot help a sharp time-locked QRS deflection and can only
blur it, while it realigns a genuinely phase-locked late component. Empirically the late/lobe
energy ratio predicts the sign, and that ratio is measurable \emph{before} choosing $\gamma$.
\item \textbf{Most of these records are lobe-dominated, one is not.} Three records lose $0.00$ to
$0.19$~dB; \texttt{sub-118/run-02}, whose late deflection is over three times its lobe deflection,
gains $+0.59$~dB non-causal and $+0.49$~dB buffered in its late window. Any corpus-level statement
about $\gamma$ is therefore a statement about the mix of records in the corpus.
\item \textbf{Latency and coordinate are separable.} The one-beat-delay penalty is roughly constant
across $\gamma$, so the streaming mode can be chosen independently of the coordinate.
\item \textbf{Figure 1 is fine as it stands.} It is drawn at $\gamma=0$ on a lobe-dominated record,
where $\gamma=0$ is the right choice; the warp would change it by $-0.19$~dB. If the warp enters
the paper it should be as a formulation containing fixed lag as a special case, not as a
performance claim.
\end{itemize}

